I'm Nora Donna with Gayle King and John  Dickerson we start with this this  morning Sri Lankans president is asking  his country's police chief and defense  minister to resign after officials  failed to act on warnings ahead of  Sunday's terror attacks at least 359  people are now reported killed in a  string of suicide bombings at churches  and hotels on Easter Sunday  police say 60 people have been arrested  in connection with the attacks the US  Embassy in Sri Lanka says it had no  prior knowledge of any threats  ambassadors said this morning she  believes terrorist groups are still  plotting possible attacks inside that  country Isis released this video  claiming that it shows a group of  suicide bombers that took part in the  attacks after pledging allegiance to the  terror group Isis has offered no other  evidence to back up its claim of  responsibility CBS News senior national  security contributor Michael Morell  joins us from Washington he's a former  deputy director and acting director of  the CIA and is the host of intelligence  matters podcast good morning Mike good  morning so two days passed before Isis  claimed responsibility what does this  tell you about what role Isis may or may  not have actually played in this bombing  so John there's two possibilities right  one is that they somehow directed the  attack the other is that they simply  inspired it the fact that it took them  two days to claim responsibility  suggests to me that it might be the  latter inspired rather than the former  directed because if they directed it  they would have known from the get-go  that that they played a role so it's  suggestive of inspiration rather than  direction but I think it's gonna take  some time for us to figure out that  exactly it was so well coordinated I  mean the number of attacks does that  suggest some foreign interference or  help so it does on the surface but I  think one thing we have to be conscious  of is that the Sri Lankan security  services are not strong I think it's  even fair to say they're inept so you  have to look at the capabilities of the  terrorist group are relative to the  security service that they're running up  against  what's interesting to me is less the  fact that they didn't do enough with the  warning they were given and more of the  fact that all of this happened right  underneath their nose and they didn't  see any of it that's more interesting to  me and it really suggests that that that  they need significant strengthening as a  security service I want to get your take  on a couple big stories out this morning  that involve Russian interference in our  elections first Jared Kushner one of the  presidents closest advisors was here in  New York he rarely does interviews and  he suggested yesterday that the multiple  investigations into Russian election  interference were more harmful to  American democracy than essentially the  original interference let's listen to  what he said you look at you know what  Russia did you know binds from facebook  ads to try to sow dissent and do it and  it's a terrible thing but I think the  investigations and and all the the  speculation has happened for the last  two years has had a much harsher impact  on democracy than a couple of Facebook  as was it just Facebook ads that Russia  was involved in no the Russians across  the whole range of activities from  stealing information and giving it to  WikiLeaks to trying to get inside state  and local voting systems to the use of  their overt media RT and Sputnik to  everything they did on social media it  was he it was a very significant and  extensive interference and and Jared is  trying to downplay this a little bit bit  by saying a few ads right we're talking  about ads that reached over a hundred  million people so this was a very  significant attack and the investigation  never would have occurred had the attack  never occurred so I think the attack is  the most significant thing here well it  was very multifaceted they were trying  to get in lots of different points and  of course the reason this is important  is not just what happened in the past  but what is happening in the future in  the New York Times has a piece this  morning that said former Department of  Homeland Security Secretary Kirsten  Neilson was told by Mick Mulvaney the  acting chief of staff not to bring up  new Russian interference efforts in  front of the president because the  president it would  the president angry what does that tell  you how significant is that so it's  absolutely clear to me and it's  absolutely clear to US intelligence  officials and we know this from public  testimony by the Director of National  Intelligence that it is highly likely  that the Russians are going to come back  and play a significant role in the 2020  election and that we should expect new  approaches new new things that they're  gonna do to try to get around the  defenses that we put in place that means  to me there needs to be a very  significant effort on the part of the US  government to defend ourselves and that  really requires the leadership of the  President and if he's not providing that  leadership then I really worry about  whether our government is going to be  ready for the attack that is almost  certainly coming all right you'll be  calling the White House to let them know  that when right away it really is some  stunning reporting in the New York Times  about whether you have a White House led  a multifaceted way to push back against  these cyber attacks and other things  Michael we're always good to have you  thank you so much
 

